1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,269
For thirty-three years, Nvidia had one boss.

2
00:00:04,259 --> 00:00:06,509
As of today, it does not.

3
00:00:06,820 --> 00:00:09,029
Jensen Huang has resigned.

4
00:00:08,980 --> 00:00:10,630
As chief executive.

5
00:00:10,580 --> 00:00:11,710
As president.

6
00:00:11,779 --> 00:00:13,710
And from his seat on the board.

7
00:00:13,859 --> 00:00:16,510
All three, effective immediately.

8
00:00:16,739 --> 00:00:19,510
The company says he left voluntarily.

9
00:00:19,539 --> 00:00:21,070
He founded Nvidia.

10
00:00:21,219 --> 00:00:24,350
He was the face of the entire AI boom.

11
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:28,589
And in less than a week, he went from untouchable to gone.

12
00:00:28,820 --> 00:00:32,429
The finance chief, Colette Kress, is now running the company.

13
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:34,670
But only as an interim boss.

14
00:00:34,899 --> 00:00:38,189
The board says it is still searching for a permanent one.

15
00:00:38,340 --> 00:00:42,789
And that is the part that should matter to anyone with a retirement account.

16
00:00:42,820 --> 00:00:47,229
The most important company in AI has no long-term leader.

17
00:00:47,539 --> 00:00:51,229
This is what happens to Nvidia without Jensen Huang.

18
00:00:52,042 --> 00:00:54,292
A quick note before we start.

19
00:00:54,121 --> 00:00:58,612
This was recorded on November 12th, hours after the announcement.

20
00:00:58,442 --> 00:01:00,932
The details will keep changing.

21
00:01:00,762 --> 00:01:03,091
The real question will not.

22
00:01:03,161 --> 00:01:05,251
Can Nvidia run without him?

23
00:01:06,150 --> 00:01:09,281
Here is what has been reported, and by whom.

24
00:01:09,671 --> 00:01:16,721
On November 8th, Nvidia said its board had opened an independent investigation into its CEO.

25
00:01:16,551 --> 00:01:28,240
That followed reports that Huang had a relationship with an executive at a company that buys Nvidia's chips, and that American officials believe she was working with Chinese intelligence.

26
00:01:28,070 --> 00:01:29,840
China denies it.

27
00:01:29,631 --> 00:01:35,200
Huang told employees, in his words, "There is no excuse for what I did."

28
00:01:35,031 --> 00:01:37,600
At first, the board backed him.

29
00:01:37,430 --> 00:01:43,441
It said it had confidence in Jensen, and no reason to believe company secrets were compromised.

30
00:01:43,350 --> 00:01:50,641
Then, this week, the New York Times reported a second relationship, this time with someone who worked for him.

31
00:01:50,430 --> 00:01:54,921
And the Wall Street Journal reported that the board was preparing to remove him.

32
00:01:54,950 --> 00:01:58,240
Those are reports, from people familiar with the matter.

33
00:01:58,471 --> 00:02:00,641
Nvidia has not confirmed them.

34
00:02:00,870 --> 00:02:04,240
And the spying claim in particular has not been proven.

35
00:02:04,471 --> 00:02:07,281
We are not going to dwell on the personal story.

36
00:02:07,510 --> 00:02:11,441
The question for anyone who owns this stock is a different one.

37
00:02:12,243 --> 00:02:16,973
Most giant companies are built so that no single person matters too much.

38
00:02:17,364 --> 00:02:19,854
Nvidia was built the opposite way.

39
00:02:20,163 --> 00:02:24,093
A typical CEO has maybe ten people reporting to them.

40
00:02:24,403 --> 00:02:27,334
Jensen Huang has more than fifty.

41
00:02:27,524 --> 00:02:28,734
No layers.

42
00:02:28,644 --> 00:02:30,014
No deputy.

43
00:02:30,084 --> 00:02:32,973
Every big decision goes through one desk.

44
00:02:32,804 --> 00:02:35,533
And the decisions were his.

45
00:02:35,524 --> 00:02:41,774
Twenty years ago he bet the company on software that let its gaming chips do scientific math.

46
00:02:41,764 --> 00:02:43,933
Wall Street hated it.

47
00:02:43,843 --> 00:02:48,574
That bet is the reason Nvidia owns the AI chip market today.

48
00:02:48,403 --> 00:02:54,493
So when investors paid five trillion dollars for this company, they were not only buying chips.

49
00:02:54,324 --> 00:02:56,894
They were buying his judgment.

50
00:02:56,883 --> 00:03:04,093
Since the investigation was announced, more than four hundred and fifty billion dollars of that value has disappeared.

51
00:03:04,003 --> 00:03:05,614
And counting.

52
00:03:05,604 --> 00:03:09,373
That is the price the market is putting on one person.

53
00:03:10,187 --> 00:03:14,356
So how does a founder with that much power go that quickly?

54
00:03:14,586 --> 00:03:17,317
Because he had less power than people think.

55
00:03:17,706 --> 00:03:23,076
At Meta, Mark Zuckerberg holds special shares that give him control of the votes.

56
00:03:23,306 --> 00:03:25,396
The board cannot fire him.

57
00:03:25,787 --> 00:03:28,116
Nvidia has nothing like that.

58
00:03:28,426 --> 00:03:31,396
Huang owned about three and a half percent of the company.

59
00:03:31,627 --> 00:03:32,917
That made him rich.

60
00:03:33,067 --> 00:03:35,076
It did not make him safe.

61
00:03:35,546 --> 00:03:43,076
Under Nvidia's own rules, a simple majority of the board can remove the CEO, with or without a reason.

62
00:03:43,387 --> 00:03:45,236
There were eleven directors.

63
00:03:45,387 --> 00:03:47,317
It would have taken six votes.

64
00:03:47,706 --> 00:03:52,276
The Wall Street Journal reported that the board was preparing to do exactly that.

65
00:03:52,586 --> 00:03:55,877
In the end, the company says, he resigned.

66
00:03:56,187 --> 00:03:59,556
And there was a reason the board could not simply wait this out.

67
00:03:59,866 --> 00:04:04,437
Nvidia needs permission from Washington to sell its best chips abroad.

68
00:04:04,747 --> 00:04:09,637
A CEO under a cloud about China is a problem for exactly that.

69
00:04:09,947 --> 00:04:14,437
A senior member of Congress had already said he was prepared to subpoena him.

70
00:04:14,826 --> 00:04:17,797
So this was never only about a private life.

71
00:04:17,786 --> 00:04:24,516
It was about whether the United States government could still trust the person running its most important chip company.

72
00:04:25,317 --> 00:04:27,487
So who gets the job for good?

73
00:04:27,798 --> 00:04:31,728
The board says it will look inside the company and outside it.

74
00:04:31,958 --> 00:04:33,807
There are three kinds of answer.

75
00:04:34,278 --> 00:04:35,528
The operator.

76
00:04:35,557 --> 00:04:36,767
Colette Kress.

77
00:04:36,997 --> 00:04:42,927
She has the job right now, as interim CEO, and she is still the finance chief as well.

78
00:04:43,237 --> 00:04:45,648
Steady, trusted by Wall Street.

79
00:04:45,877 --> 00:04:49,327
The open question is whether "interim" becomes permanent.

80
00:04:49,877 --> 00:04:50,927
The engineer.

81
00:04:51,158 --> 00:04:54,288
Ian Buck joined Nvidia in 2004.

82
00:04:54,437 --> 00:05:03,728
He was a key architect of the software that made the AI boom possible, and today he runs the part of Nvidia that sells to the biggest AI customers.

83
00:05:03,958 --> 00:05:08,288
If you want someone who understands why Nvidia wins, it is him.

84
00:05:08,598 --> 00:05:11,088
But he has never run a whole company.

85
00:05:11,557 --> 00:05:12,848
The outsider.

86
00:05:12,838 --> 00:05:19,567
Press reports have named people like Lisa Su, who runs rival AMD, and Rene Haas of Arm.

87
00:05:19,798 --> 00:05:21,567
Proven chief executives.

88
00:05:21,798 --> 00:05:26,288
But it would mean handing the keys to someone who was not there for any of it.

89
00:05:26,757 --> 00:05:30,208
And then there is the answer Newsweek put in a headline this week.

90
00:05:30,517 --> 00:05:32,447
Who could replace Jensen Huang?

91
00:05:32,838 --> 00:05:33,887
Nobody.

92
00:05:34,701 --> 00:05:36,232
That sounds bleak.

93
00:05:36,541 --> 00:05:40,911
So let us look at what actually happened to three other companies.

94
00:05:41,021 --> 00:05:43,432
Intel, 2018.

95
00:05:43,262 --> 00:05:47,672
The CEO was pushed out over a relationship with an employee.

96
00:05:47,461 --> 00:05:50,312
The stock barely moved that day.

97
00:05:50,141 --> 00:05:54,951
But over the next few years, Intel lost its lead in making chips.

98
00:05:54,781 --> 00:05:57,351
The trouble was already underway.

99
00:05:57,182 --> 00:06:01,351
Losing the boss did not cause it, and did not fix it.

100
00:06:01,182 --> 00:06:03,672
McDonald's, 2019.

101
00:06:03,541 --> 00:06:06,872
Same reason, same outcome for the CEO.

102
00:06:06,701 --> 00:06:09,432
The stock dropped about five percent.

103
00:06:09,341 --> 00:06:13,831
Then the new boss carried on, and the shares went on to new highs.

104
00:06:13,901 --> 00:06:15,031
And Apple.

105
00:06:15,021 --> 00:06:21,112
In 2011, Steve Jobs stepped down, and plenty of people said the company was finished.

106
00:06:20,942 --> 00:06:24,591
His replacement was an operations man named Tim Cook.

107
00:06:24,622 --> 00:06:27,831
Apple is worth about ten times more today.

108
00:06:28,141 --> 00:06:29,672
The lesson is simple.

109
00:06:29,822 --> 00:06:36,552
It depends on whether the company's edge lives in one person's head, or in the system that person built.

110
00:06:37,451 --> 00:06:40,021
So here is what to look for.

111
00:06:39,930 --> 00:06:40,781
One.

112
00:06:40,810 --> 00:06:43,661
How long the word "interim" lasts.

113
00:06:43,690 --> 00:06:47,620
An interim boss means the board does not have its answer yet.

114
00:06:47,850 --> 00:06:50,980
The longer that word stays, the more it tells you.

115
00:06:51,370 --> 00:06:52,221
Two.

116
00:06:52,250 --> 00:06:54,100
Whether the engineers stay.

117
00:06:54,411 --> 00:06:56,740
Nvidia's lead is its people.

118
00:06:56,971 --> 00:07:02,261
If the top technical names start leaving, that tells you more than any press release.

119
00:07:02,571 --> 00:07:03,461
Three.

120
00:07:03,451 --> 00:07:05,341
November 17th.

121
00:07:05,370 --> 00:07:07,380
Nvidia reports its earnings.

122
00:07:07,610 --> 00:07:13,180
They will be the first results the company has ever delivered without Jensen Huang in charge.

123
00:07:13,211 --> 00:07:20,021
For thirty years, Jensen Huang told his employees the company was thirty days from going out of business.

124
00:07:19,810 --> 00:07:22,980
He was trying to prepare them for everything.

125
00:07:22,850 --> 00:07:27,141
He never told them what to do if the thing they lost was him.
